{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Field-Based Transfer and Coaching","title":"Equity Audit in Field Contexts","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting equity audit in field contexts to equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"04","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_equity_audit_in_field_contexts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/07/03"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Equity Audit in Field Contexts\n\n![Equity Audit in Field Contexts textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/07-04-equity-audit-in-field-contexts.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA generic risk statement, \"this tool might not work for every student,\" protects no one, because it names no student, no harm, and no safeguard. An equity audit replaces that vagueness with a context-specific risk register: for this tool, in this placement, with these students, here is who is most likely to be harmed, how, and what we will do about it. The shift from generic to specific is the whole skill. \"Might not be perfect\" cannot be acted on; \"the feedback model flags multilingual phrasing as errors, and 11 of my 27 students are multilingual\" can.\n\nThis section treats the equity-oriented evaluation matrix as a tool for converting abstract caution into context-bound, monitorable risks. A risk you cannot tie to a named group, a mechanism, and a check is not yet an audit finding.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Convert generic risk statements into context-specific risks tied to a named group, a mechanism, and your actual placement constraints.\n- Treat vendor fairness claims as unverified hypotheses and write the questions they leave open.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move, replacing AI-generated generic risks with placement-specific ones.\n- Produce an evaluation matrix that pairs each risk with an affected group, a safeguard, and a monitoring check.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nVendors and AI assistants both produce risk language that sounds responsible and commits to nothing. \"Bias-tested and fair for all learners\" is a marketing claim, not evidence; it does not say which groups were tested, on what data, or whether your students resemble them. Accepting it ends the audit before it starts.\n\nThe audit begins when you treat that claim as a hypothesis and ask the questions it dodges. A fairness claim with no test population named is an open question, not a closed case. The matrix exists to hold those open questions next to your real roster, so the decision to adopt rests on what you can verify about your students rather than what the vendor asserts about everyone.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn equity audit is disaggregation applied to risk. A whole-class average hides the students a tool fails; the audit's job is to break the class into the groups whose experience of the tool differs and ask what happens to each. The unit of analysis is never \"the students\"; it is \"the 11 multilingual learners,\" \"the 3 students using read-aloud,\" \"the 9 without reliable home access.\"\n\nEach finding has three required parts. The **affected group** names who, specifically and by count. The **mechanism** names how the tool produces the harm (a model trained on standard-English essays flags non-standard phrasing). The **safeguard** names what you will do (teacher review of flagged multilingual work) and the **monitoring check** names when you will confirm it worked (two weeks after rollout). A risk missing any part is incomplete.\n\nThree lenses drive the disaggregation:\n\n- **[Critical edtech studies](https://doi.org/10.4067/s0718-50492025000100248)** (Selwyn) supplies the core move: ask whose interests the tool was built around and which students sit outside that default. A model trained mostly on standard English has a built-in default that your multilingual students fall outside of.\n- **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** asks what the tool does with the data it gathers, and whether its training data represented your students at all. Underrepresentation in training is a mechanism, not a footnote.\n- **[TPACK](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x)** keeps the audit tethered to learning: a tool that fails a subgroup on the actual content goal is failing, regardless of how engaging it is for the majority.\n\nA lens has done its work when it produced a specific risk row, not a general worry.\n\n> **Recent evidence:** Both of this section's anchor risks are documented findings, not hypotheticals. On facial recognition, Buolamwini & Gebru's [Gender Shades](http://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a/buolamwini18a.pdf) study (2018) found commercial face-analysis error rates up to 34.7% for darker-skinned women versus 0.8% for lighter-skinned men, and NIST's [FRVT Part 3: Demographic Effects](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2019/NIST.IR.8280.pdf) report (2019) found demographic differentials in the majority of algorithms tested, with elevated false positives for African American and Asian faces in one-to-many matching. On essay scoring, [Wilson & Huang (2024)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asw.2024.100815) found evidence of bias in automated essay scores for English language learners.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A vendor page states the tool is 'bias-tested and fair for all learners.' How should the audit treat this claim?\" explanation=\"As an unverified hypothesis, not a finding. The claim names no test population, no data, and no whether your students were represented. The audit logs it as open and records the questions it leaves open: which groups were tested, on what data, and were multilingual learners (or your specific group) represented.\" options='[{\"text\":\"As an unverified hypothesis, logging the questions it leaves open\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"As evidence that satisfies the equity requirement\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"As a reason to skip disaggregating your own roster\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare a generic risk to an audited one. The generic version is unactionable:\n\n> The tool might not be perfect for every student.\n\nIt names no group, no mechanism, no safeguard. The audited version is a usable finding:\n\n> The feedback model was trained mostly on standard-English essays. Of my 27 students, 11 are multilingual learners whose phrasing the tool may flag as errors. Safeguard: teacher review of all flagged multilingual phrasing before students see comments. Check: compare flag rates for multilingual and monolingual writers two weeks after rollout.\n\nThe second version can be acted on, assigned, and verified. It also handles the vendor's \"fair for all learners\" claim correctly by logging it as unverified and recording the question it leaves open: which groups were tested, on what data, and were multilingual learners represented.\n\nSet side by side, the structure becomes clear:\n\n| Element | Generic risk | Audited risk |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Affected group | \"every student\" | 11 multilingual learners (of 27) |\n| Mechanism | none stated | model trained mostly on standard-English essays |\n| Safeguard | none | teacher review of flagged multilingual phrasing |\n| Monitoring check | none | compare flag rates by group at two weeks |\n\n### Annotated Judgment Call: Auditing an AI Writing-Feedback Tool\n\nSofia audits an AI writing-feedback tool before her placement adopts it for 27 students.\n\n1. **Before (generic risk):** \"The tool might not be perfect for every student.\"\n2. **Context-specific risk:** \"The feedback model was trained mostly on standard-English essays. Of my 27 students, 11 are multilingual learners whose phrasing the tool may flag as errors.\"\n3. **Vendor claim handled:** The vendor page says the tool is \"bias-tested and fair for all learners.\" Sofia logs this as an unverified claim and writes the question it leaves open: which groups were tested, on what data, and were multilingual learners represented.\n4. **AI output grounded:** An AI assistant generated a risk column that applied to any school. She replaced each generic line with a risk tied to her actual students, shared device cart, and district data policy.\n5. **After (recommendation with safeguards):** Adopt with safeguards. Each risk is paired with the affected group, a safeguard (teacher review of flagged multilingual phrasing), and a monitoring check two weeks after rollout.\n\nA colleague can now trust the recommendation because every risk names who is affected and how it will be watched.\n\nBuild a real audit row from a generic worry. Predict the next part each time before the reveal supplies it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Turning a generic risk into an audit row\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You start with: \\\"The tool might not be perfect for every student.\\\" What does an audit row require first that this sentence lacks?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A more confident tone\",\"A named affected group with a count\",\"A longer description of the tool\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Correct. The first required part is the affected group, named specifically and by count: \\\"11 multilingual learners (of 27),\\\" not \\\"every student.\\\"\"},{\"reveal\":\"Add the mechanism, how the tool produces the harm: \\\"the feedback model was trained mostly on standard-English essays, so it may flag non-standard phrasing as errors.\\\" A risk with no mechanism cannot be acted on.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Add the safeguard: \\\"teacher review of all flagged multilingual phrasing before students see comments.\\\" The safeguard must address the named mechanism, not your convenience.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Add the monitoring check with a date: \\\"compare flag rates for multilingual and monolingual writers two weeks after rollout.\\\" Now the row is a usable finding: group, mechanism, safeguard, dated check. Only now is \\\"adopt with safeguards\\\" defensible.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Your placement is considering an AI attendance system that uses facial recognition, in a class whose roster is majority Black and Latino. Write one audit row: affected group, mechanism, safeguard, and dated check.\" answer=\"Affected group: the majority-Black-and-Latino roster, because facial-recognition error rates are documented to be higher for darker-skinned students. Mechanism: the model training data underrepresented these groups, so misidentification is structurally more likely for them. Safeguard: a manual roster check whenever the system fails to match, and no tardy mark triggered by the system alone. Monitoring check: review misidentification logs by student two weeks after rollout and compare rates across groups, reconsidering adoption if a gap appears.\"></self-explain>\n\nFor each risk, name the group before the harm and the safeguard before the recommendation. A recommendation to adopt is only as good as the safeguards attached to its risks. Sofia's audit follows the rule: she does not write \"adopt\" until each risk carries an affected group, a safeguard, and a check, which is why \"adopt with safeguards\" is a defensible recommendation rather than a hopeful one.\n\nBefore you submit an audit, three checks should pass:\n\n1. **Specificity check:** Does every risk name an actual group by count, a mechanism, and a placement constraint, or do some rows apply to any school anywhere?\n2. **Claim check:** Did you treat each vendor fairness claim as a hypothesis and record the question it leaves open, rather than accepting it as evidence?\n3. **Monitoring check:** Does every risk you accepted carry a safeguard and a dated check, so adoption is conditional and watched, not final?\n\nIf any risk lacks a group, a mechanism, or a check, the audit is not yet actionable.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating a vendor's fairness claim as a finding; it is an advertisement until you know the test population. The second is leaving risks generic enough to apply to any school, which feels thorough but commits to nothing and protects no specific student. The third is accepting an AI-generated risk column wholesale; these read comprehensive but float free of your roster, and the audit only becomes real when each line is rewritten against your actual students, device cart, and data policy.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the matrix to a different tool. Your placement is considering an AI attendance system that uses facial recognition to log entry. The generic risk (\"it might misidentify some students\") is useless. Disaggregate.\n\nAffected group: facial-recognition error rates are documented to be higher for darker-skinned students, and your placement's roster is majority Black and Latino. Mechanism: the model's training data underrepresented these groups, so misidentification is structurally more likely for them. Safeguard: a manual roster check whenever the system fails to match, and no consequence (such as a tardy mark) triggered by the system alone. Monitoring check: review misidentification logs by student after two weeks and compare rates across groups. The recommendation that follows is not a thumbs-up; it is \"adopt only with the manual override and the disparity check, and reconsider if the two-week logs show a gap.\" The walkthrough repeats the structure: name the group, name the mechanism, attach a safeguard, schedule the check.\n\n> **Legal floor:** Biometric attendance data on minors is not only a fairness question; it implicates FERPA (education records) and, for students under 13, COPPA. As of 2026, the FTC's [amended COPPA rule](https://www.loeb.com/en/insights/publications/2025/05/childrens-online-privacy-in-2025-the-amended-coppa-rule) (finalized January 2025, with full compliance required by April 22, 2026) explicitly classifies biometric identifiers, including facial templates, as covered personal information requiring verifiable parental consent. The audit's monitoring check therefore also confirms legal basis and consent, not only error-rate disparity.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read an audit, keep a two-column note: **named group and mechanism** on the left, **safeguard and check** on the right. A row with the left side empty is a slogan; a row with the right side empty is an alarm with no response. A strong audit has both columns filled for every risk it raises.\n\nA strong reader of an equity audit can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each risk, which actual group is affected and through what mechanism?\n2. Which vendor claims were accepted as evidence, and which were correctly logged as open questions?\n3. What safeguard and dated check make the recommendation conditional rather than blind?\n4. What would change in the matrix if this exact tool were adopted in a placement with a different student population?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nAn equity audit is strong when a colleague could adopt the tool using only your matrix and know exactly who to watch and when. That requires every risk to carry its group, mechanism, safeguard, and check, and every vendor claim to be marked verified or open. Clean tables are the last layer; a matrix that looks rigorous but contains risks that could describe any school has skipped the specificity that makes it an audit rather than a checklist.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like this: \"I recommend adopting with safeguards because each risk names a real group and a check, not a general caution. I rejected the AI-generated risk column, because risks that fit any school protect none of mine.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same audit to a placement with the opposite profile: a tool that works well for multilingual learners but was never tested on students with visual impairments, in a class that includes two students using screen readers. The disaggregation still drives the audit, but the affected group, the mechanism (incompatibility with screen-reader navigation), and the safeguard (an accessible alternate path) all change. If you can rebuild the matrix around a different vulnerable group rather than reusing the multilingual rows, you understand equity auditing as a method rather than a single finding.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/07-04-equity-audit-in-field-contexts-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"07.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes for CAT 531 07.04. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_equity_audit_in_field_contexts\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure each risk names an actual group and mechanism, each vendor claim is marked verified or open, and each accepted risk carries a safeguard and a dated check. That is what separates an audit from a general caution.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\", \"cat531_supplement_equity_audit_in_field_contexts\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x). Mishra & Koehler (2006). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. Selwyn (2016), https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160; Selwyn, Rivera-Vargas & Herrera-Urizar (2025), https://doi.org/10.4067/s0718-50492025000100248\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017); Williamson, Macgilchrist & Potter (2023), Re-examining AI, automation and datafication in education, https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2023.2167830\n- Buolamwini & Gebru (2018). Gender Shades: Intersectional accuracy disparities in commercial gender classification. http://proceedings.mlr.press/v81/buolamwini18a/buolamwini18a.pdf\n- Grother, Ngan & Hanaoka, NIST (2019). FRVT Part 3: Demographic Effects (NISTIR 8280). https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2019/NIST.IR.8280.pdf\n- Wilson & Huang (2024). Validity of automated essay scores for elementary-age English language learners: Evidence of bias? Assessing Writing, 60. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asw.2024.100815\n- FTC amended COPPA rule (2025), biometric identifiers. https://www.loeb.com/en/insights/publications/2025/05/childrens-online-privacy-in-2025-the-amended-coppa-rule\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_equity_audit_in_field_contexts","stem":"A candidate is auditing an AI writing-feedback tool for equity before her placement adopts it. Which entry in the audit matrix is a context-specific equity risk rather than a generic worry?","options":["The tool might not be perfect for every student.","The tool's feedback model was trained mostly on standard-English essays, and 11 of the 27 students in this placement are multilingual learners whose phrasing it may flag as errors.","Some teachers do not like new technology.","The tool could be slow if the internet is down."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A context-specific equity risk names a real subgroup in this placement and the mechanism by which the tool could disadvantage them. The other entries are generic, off-topic, or about reliability rather than equity.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_generic_risk_for_equity","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_equity_audit_in_field_contexts","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The vendor's equity page says the tool is bias-tested and fair for all learners. How should that claim enter the audit matrix?","options":["As a settled fact in the equity column, since the vendor tested it.","As an unverified vendor claim that needs evidence: which groups were tested, on what data, and whether the candidate's multilingual learners were represented.","As a reason to skip the audit for this tool.","As proof the tool needs no monitoring in this classroom."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A vendor's fairness claim is a marketing statement until the audit asks who was tested and whether this classroom's students were represented. Recording it as settled fact hides the equity question.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_vendor_claim_as_audit","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"The candidate asks an AI assistant to generate the equity risk column for the matrix. It returns risks that apply to any school. What is the defensible use of that output?","options":["Paste the generic risks into the matrix because the column is now filled.","Use the generic risks as prompts, then replace each with a risk tied to this placement's specific student groups, devices, and policies.","Trust the AI risks because they cover many districts.","Remove the equity column since AI already addressed it."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Generic AI risks are prompts, not the audit. An equity audit only does its job when each risk is grounded in this placement's actual context. The other options either accept generic output or drop the analysis.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_ai_generic_fills_matrix","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_equity_audit_in_field_contexts_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"What evidence would let a colleague trust the candidate's recommendation to adopt the tool with safeguards?","options":["The matrix has every cell filled in.","Each named risk is paired with the affected student group, the safeguard chosen, and a monitoring check the candidate will run after rollout.","The tool was the cheapest option reviewed.","The candidate liked the tool's interface."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A trustworthy equity recommendation links each risk to who is affected, the safeguard, and a post-rollout check. A fully filled grid, price, or preference does not show the reasoning held up.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_4_filled_grid_equals_equity","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"41f2a0550e06aa392cd1ed493dc2f02d1a2ea66e0488b5ecc80321ca636369d5","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}